type: software
abstract: >-
  Research record (work in progress) proposing that recurring problems receive shared, verifiable
  identities built from confirmed "same problem" links, used across applications. Includes a dated
  protocol, code and results. Confirmed claim: on new AskUbuntu questions (2020-2024, never used in the
  exploratory phase), using previously recorded duplicate relations increased the retrieval of discussions
  from the marked family among five suggestions; an interleaved combination (plain semantic search first,
  alternating with a relation-based ranking that includes a popularity term) went from 1.61% to 2.57% of
  new questions (+59% relative), keeping the first suggestion identical to plain search. Relations without
  the popularity term gave +41%. Whether the relation-based ranking can safely choose the first suggestion
  was not resolved: the official outcome of the confirmation is inconclusive because the pre-registered
  judge failed its controls. Version 0.2.0 adds rounds 9 to 11. In two pre-registered rounds, a local
  model answered new questions with retrieved context and blind judges compared the answers: answers were
  preferred when the context brought the marked family (AskUbuntu); on Super User questions without a marked duplicate, removing the
  popularity term improved answers relative to the interleaved method, and across eligible Super User
  questions the network without that term stayed within 3 points of plain search. A benefit over plain search was not shown, and a gate to decide in
  advance when to use the network could not be calibrated for its signal, grid and requirements. Judgements are preferences, not problems
  verified as solved; savings of work were not measured. Negative and inconclusive results, deviations
  and an erratum are reported. Code: MIT; documentation and results: CC BY 4.0.
keywords:
  - information retrieval
  - duplicate question detection
  - community question answering
  - problem identity
  - knowledge sharing
  - AI curation
  - pre-registration
  - retrieval-augmented generation
  - LLM evaluation
